		<r3d:repositoryName language="eng">Ozone Mapping and Profiler Suite</r3d:repositoryName>
				<r3d:additionalName language="eng">Measuring global ozone, aerosols, and reflectivity</r3d:additionalName>
				<r3d:additionalName language="eng">OMPS</r3d:additionalName>
				<r3d:repositoryUrl>https://ozoneaq.gsfc.nasa.gov/omps/</r3d:repositoryUrl>
				<r3d:repositoryIdentifier>
			<r3d:repositoryIdentifierType>FAIRsharing</r3d:repositoryIdentifierType>
			<r3d:repositoryIdentifierValue>10.25504/FAIRsharing.24eb84</r3d:repositoryIdentifierValue>
		</r3d:repositoryIdentifier>
				<r3d:description language="eng">The Ozone Mapping and Profiler Suite measures the ozone layer in our upper atmosphere—tracking the status of global ozone distributions, including the ‘ozone hole.’ It also monitors ozone levels in the troposphere, the lowest layer of our atmosphere. OMPS extends out 40-year long record ozone layer measurements while also providing improved vertical resolution compared to previous operational instruments. Closer to the ground, OMPS’s measurements of harmful ozone improve air quality monitoring and when combined with cloud predictions; help to create the Ultraviolet Index, a guide to safe levels of sunlight exposure. OMPS has two sensors, both new designs, composed of three advanced hyperspectralimaging spectrometers.The three spectrometers: a downward-looking nadir mapper, nadir profiler and limb profiler. The entire OMPS suite currently fly on board the Suomi NPP spacecraft and are scheduled to fly on the JPSS-2 satellite mission. NASA will provide the OMPS-Limb profiler.</r3d:description>
